  "correct_approach": "First apply a legality check, then simulate each candidate line turn by turn. For each turn, update enemy HP after every card, update player block, resolve the enemy intent if the enemy survives, and continue until a verified lethal card is played. For the selected line: turn 1 play Transcend, costing 3, dealing 24 and giving 18 block; enemy HP becomes 30 and the 9 attack is fully blocked. Turn 2 play Bludgeon, costing 3, dealing 28; enemy HP becomes 2 and the 12 attack would be relevant only if the player were not killing the enemy next. Turn 3 play Strike, costing 1, dealing 6; enemy HP reaches 0 before its guard intent, so combat ends with player HP 80. The response should therefore include turns 1, 2, and 3.",
  "key_insight": "Never declare a kill based on intended damage; explicitly verify enemy_hp_after = enemy_hp_before - effective damage and require it to be less than or equal to zero. After every nonlethal turn, continue simulation using the exact next hand and intent. Earlier damage is valuable because it can eliminate future attacks, but that benefit only exists when the damage is actually sufficient to reach a verified lethal turn.",
